# /demo was the scenario page. It is now a section of the homepage, so the
# target carries a fragment: Cloudflare's redirects support them in the
# destination ("Can include fragments, query strings, splats, and placeholders"),
# and only a line-leading # is a comment. The sitemap published /demo/ too.
#
# The cost, recorded because it is a real one: a destination fragment replaces
# whatever fragment the reader arrived with, so /demo#flip-a-byte lands on the
# section rather than on that scenario. Dropping the fragment here would invert
# the trade — deep links would keep working and every bare /demo would land at
# the top of the homepage, a band above the thing it asked for. Bare /demo is
# what the sitemap, the old nav item, and any bookmark all point at. The one
# deep link the site itself published was /demo#flip-a-byte on the homepage, and
# the commit that folded the page deleted it, so the cost falls only on a link
# somebody else wrote.
/demo /#when-something-goes-wrong 308
/demo/ /#when-something-goes-wrong 308
# /compare was the comparison page. Its table and its five project notes are now
# the two bands under "How it compares" on /product, so the destination carries
# that section's id.
#
# The fragment costs less here than it did for /demo: /compare published no deep
# links of its own — it had no ids on any of its four sections — so nothing that
# arrives with a fragment can have it replaced. The sitemap had already given
# /compare/ to crawlers, and it was a nav item on every page of the site, which
# is the widest inbound surface any route here has had.
/compare /product/#how-it-compares 308
/compare/ /product/#how-it-compares 308
